    - Build block 1 as a wall-framed R1xC1 grid of S1xS1 rooms. Place one 1-pixel doorway per internal wall segment. Choose one exit doorway on the outer wall.
    - Build block 2 similarly with S2xS2 rooms and its own exit doorway.
    - Place blocks on a bg grid with 2-8 pixel gap between them, with exits facing each other.
    - In the common branch, offset the opposing exits along the perpendicular axis
      so the unobstructed middle space becomes a rectangular all-shortest-path fill
      region. Choose the offset only within the common span covered by facing
      square frames on both sides. In the minority branch, align exits and draw a
      1-pixel corridor.
    - Run BFS to verify a connected path exists from every room to every other room and through the inter-block connector.
    - Choose one room in each block for the endpoint; fill it with paint color (clip to grid boundary if at edge).
    - Verify the solver produces a non-trivial output (>=6 rooms on route, >=1 room off route).
spec:
  grid:
    shape: square, side = 2*N+1 where N is bar count
    background: 7
  bars:
    count: 3-5
    placement: one per odd column (1, 3, ..., 2N-1), evenly spaced with gap=1 bg column
    alignment: bottom-aligned, 1-cell wide, contiguous
    height: 1 to side-1 per bar; not all bars same height [observed]
    colors: all distinct, each differs from background (7); any non-bg color allowed
  transform:
    columns: unchanged between input and output
    color_rotation: each bar gets the color of the previous bar (cyclic right shift)
    height_rotation: each bar gets the height of the next bar (cyclic left shift)

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (constraints are simple enough for forward generation)
  steps:
    1. Pick N in [3,5]; grid side S = 2*N+1
    2. Sample N distinct non-7 colors from 0-9
    3. Sample N heights from [1, S-1] ensuring not all identical
    4. Place bars at columns 1,3,...,2N-1, each bottom-aligned with its color and height
    5. Fill remaining cells with 7
  output_derivation:
    For bar i, set color = bars[(i-1) % N].color, height = bars[(i+1) % N].height
